From: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: qcom: do not release a PHY whose power-on failed

qcom_pcie_phy_power_on() unwinds a failed phy_power_on() by calling
qcom_pcie_phy_power_off(), which walks the whole port list - including the port
that just failed, and every port the loop has not reached yet.

phy_power_off() decrements phy->power_count unconditionally and only invokes the
provider's ->power_off at a count of 1, while phy_power_on() only invokes
->power_on at a count of 0. Releasing a reference that was never acquired
therefore drives the count to -1, and the next phy_power_on() quietly skips
powering the PHY up: no error, no message, just a PHY that is never initialised
again. The PHY core already unwinds the failed acquisition itself, so this
release is both wrong and unnecessary.

On a single-port controller the very first failure is enough to reach -1. It is
normally invisible because a failed host init at probe time is fatal anyway, but
it becomes reachable the moment anything retries host init at runtime, as system
resume does.

[Local note, not for the upstream message: the other runtime retry that makes
 this reachable here is the link-recovery hook carried as 0978, which is a local
 experiment and must not be referenced in anything sent upstream.]

[Prior art, checked 2026-11-21. No report or patch for THIS function was found:
 mainline master still has qcom_pcie_phy_power_on() calling the whole-list
 qcom_pcie_phy_power_off() on a failed phy_power_on(), and still returns bare
 from the phy_set_mode_ext() failure leaving earlier ports powered. But the
 mechanism is already conceded upstream, which is worth citing when sending:

 - Vladimir Oltean, reviewing "scsi: ufs: qcom: Drop the PHY power_count check"
   (March 2026), stated exactly this underflow: "If 0, the power_count would
   become negative after phy_power_off(), and a subsequent phy_power_on() would
   merely bump it back to 0". The patch's author - Manivannan Sadhasivam, who
   also maintains qcom PCIe - accepted it and withdrew his own patch ("let's
   drop this patch and proceed with your state tracking patch instead"). So the
   reviewer most likely to see this one has already agreed the underflow is real.

 - The counter-precedent to expect: "PCI: tegra: Unwind partial PHY ..."
   (Pengpeng Hou, August 2026) made the structurally identical unwind fix for
   tegra and was rejected by Thierry Reding - "If this ever happened, it'd mean
   that either the system is improperly designed, badly configured in device
   tree or somehow broken. In either case the unwind isn't really going to save
   you."

   That objection answers a LEAK argument, and it is fair against one. It does
   not answer this patch, and the difference has to be led with rather than
   defended later: the damage here is not a PHY left powered, it is a refcount
   driven BELOW zero, which outlives the failed attempt and silently disables
   the next successful-looking phy_power_on(). Frame it as state corruption
   reachable from system resume, not as cleanup hygiene.

 Message-ids were not recovered - these were read via a list mirror, so look
 them up on lore.kernel.org before putting Link: tags in a submission.

 Also in flight near this code: a "PCI: qcom: Add link retention support" series
 (v3, July 2026) that itself mentions leaking PHY power-on/clock/regulator counts
 on probe deferral. Rebase against current -next before sending; this may
 collide.]

Unwind with list_for_each_entry_continue_reverse() so only the ports that were
successfully powered on are powered off, and fail the phy_set_mode_ext() case
the same way instead of leaking the ports already acquired.

Fixes: a2fbecdbbb9d ("PCI: qcom: Add support for parsing the new Root Port binding")
Signed-off-by: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>

[Submitted 2026-11-21 to linux-pci/linux-arm-msm as
 <20261121113721.7OL6u%mhudan21@gmail.com>. NOT reviewed by a human yet - the
 only reply is from sashiko-bot@kernel.org, the Linux Foundation's automated
 reviewer (gemini-4-argon). It found one Low item, the missing Fixes: tag above,
 and nothing else; it did not dispute the mechanism or the fix. A bot's silence
 on the rest is not review, and its one finding is not rejection.

 The Fixes: target was NOT taken on the bot's word. Read a2fbecdbbb9d directly:
 before it, qcom_pcie_host_init() called phy_power_on(pcie->phy) and simply
 returned on failure. That commit introduced BOTH qcom_pcie_phy_power_off() and
 the call to it from the phy_power_on() failure path - that call is the release
 of a never-acquired reference this patch removes. So it introduced the bug
 rather than inheriting it, and the tag is correct.

 v1 applied cleanly to linux-next/HEAD (909ef7a9a30f) in the bot's baseline run.
 A v2 carrying only the Fixes: tag is prepared but deliberately NOT sent yet:
 respinning within days for a bot's nit risks a v3 once a human replies.]
---
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
@@ -1262,16 +1262,29 @@
 	list_for_each_entry(port, &pcie->ports, list) {
 		ret = phy_set_mode_ext(port->phy, PHY_MODE_PCIE, PHY_MODE_PCIE_RC);
 		if (ret)
-			return ret;
+			goto err_power_off;
 
 		ret = phy_power_on(port->phy);
-		if (ret) {
-			qcom_pcie_phy_power_off(pcie);
-			return ret;
-		}
+		if (ret)
+			goto err_power_off;
 	}
 
 	return 0;
+
+err_power_off:
+	/*
+	 * Only release what was actually acquired. phy_power_off() decrements
+	 * phy->power_count unconditionally and invokes the provider's callback
+	 * only at 1, while phy_power_on() invokes it only at 0, so releasing a
+	 * port whose phy_power_on() failed (or was never reached) drives the
+	 * count negative - after which the next phy_power_on() silently skips
+	 * powering the PHY up at all. The PHY core already unwinds the failed
+	 * acquisition itself.
+	 */
+	list_for_each_entry_continue_reverse(port, &pcie->ports, list)
+		phy_power_off(port->phy);
+
+	return ret;
 }
 
 static int qcom_pcie_host_init(struct dw_pcie_rp *pp)
